<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psamtik II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 595–589 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Psammetichus II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Psamtik II was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 26th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>. In 593 BCE he sent an army, including Greek and Carian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who left graffiti at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>, deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> against the kingdom of Kush. The raid contributed to the shift of the Kushite centre southwards from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii.jpg" alt="Psamtik II" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OliBac · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Psamtik_II_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Psamtik II, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Psammetichus, was a king of the Saite-based Twenty-sixth Dynasty and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a>. His prenomen, Nefer-Ib-Re, means beautiful is the heart of Re. A stela from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> dated to the third year of his reign records a heavy defeat inflicted on the kingdom of Kush, and the Egyptian army marched as far south as the Third or even the Fourth Cataract. It was the first confrontation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> since the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> stela says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> rose against the army at Pnubs and capitulated before a single arrow was loosed, and that four thousand two hundred men were brought back as prisoners. A Greek graffito on the colossal statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> records that those who sailed with the king came as far as the river permits, the foreign-speaking troops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a>, led by Potasimto and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> by Amasis. The campaign weakened Kush, and the Nubian rulers moved their capital south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> to Meroe, although most historians agree this was not the only reason. Elephantine remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s southern border, and the names and royal emblems of the Kushite 25th Dynasty kings were hacked out of their monuments in Egypt.</p><p>Psamtik was also a builder. A pair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> more than 21.79 metres high stood in the temple of Heliopolis, and Augustus later had one of them, today the Obelisk of Montecitorio, brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 10 BCE. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> he built a kiosk, the oldest known monument on the island, and at Hibis in the El-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharga-oasis">Kharga Oasis</a> he founded a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Mut and Khonsu, whose decoration was completed only under the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> and possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Psamtik II died in 589 BCE and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a>, born to Queen Takhut. Another of their children, Ankhnesneferibre, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a> until the Persian conquest of 525 BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, ambassadors from Elis visited the king, and the wise men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> told them that the Olympic Games should admit only strangers, since the Eleans would favour their own townsmen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Psamtik II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the loss of Egypt to the Assyrians in the seventh century BCE, power was concentrated at Napata, and after an Egyptian army under <b>Psamtik II</b> raided Nubia around 593 BCE the political centre moved progressively southward to Meroë.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
